-- title:
--   Finiteness of the eigenform coefficient ring over ℤ
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number and let $g$ be a cusp form of weight $2$ for $\Gamma_0(M)$. Assume $g$ is a normalized eigenform in the sense of the project predicate `IsNormalizedEigenform`, i.e. writing $a_n =$ the $n$-th coefficient of the $q$-expansion (at width $1$) of $g$: $a_1 = 1$; $a_{mn} = a_m a_n$ whenever $m$ and $n$ are coprime; $a_{p^{r+2}} = a_p a_{p^{r+1}} - p\,a_{p^r}$ for every prime $p \nmid M$ and every $r$; and $a_{p^{r+2}} = a_p a_{p^{r+1}}$ for every prime $p \mid M$ and every $r$. Assume furthermore the hypothesis `PrimeCoeffsIntegral`: for every prime $\ell$ there exists an element of the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $\mathbb{C}$ whose image in $\mathbb{C}$ is $a_\ell$. From this hypothesis one extracts, for each prime $\ell$, a chosen such element `eigenLift`, and `eigenCoeffRing` is the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $\mathbb{C}$ generated by the set of these chosen lifts. The conclusion is that `eigenCoeffRing` is a finite $\mathbb{Z}$-module.
--
--   This is the standard finiteness statement that the ring generated by the prime-indexed Fourier coefficients of a weight-$2$ normalized eigenform is an order in a number field, here in the form 'module-finite over $\mathbb{Z}$'. It is used to obtain finite-dimensionality of the $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra generated by the coefficients of $g$ ([`CuspForm.finiteDimensional_adjoin_qCoeff`](thm.html#CuspForm.finiteDimensional_adjoin_qCoeff)), which in turn supports the passage from an eigenform to residual representations over finite fields.
